{
  "mutual-fund": {
    "heading": "Separate sales charges, annual expenses and lost growth",
    "intro": "Hold $10,000 initially, $100 at each month end, ten years and a hypothetical 5% gross annual return fixed. Change only the charge named in each row. The no-charge balance is a model counterfactual, not an available investment.",
    "columns": [
      "Scenario",
      "No-charge balance",
      "Net redemption value",
      "Charges and lost-growth effect"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "The entry charge reduces the starting investment and every new contribution. At 3%, investor deposits are $22,000 over ten years but $660 is withheld as entry charges before investment. The remaining money still faces the modeled annual expense.",
      "The annual net growth factor is (1 + gross return) × (1 − annual expense). Monthly deposits use its equivalent monthly rate and arrive at month end. This is a simplified growth convention, not the fund’s exact daily expense accrual.",
      "The 2% exit-charge case deducts 2% of the entire final pre-exit balance. Actual deferred sales charges can use different bases, schedules and exemptions; use the prospectus rather than assuming this convention matches your fund.",
      "The gap to the no-charge balance includes both deducted charges and foregone growth. It is not a total of cash fees billed to you. The pre-exit schedule and the final redemption metric differ when an exit charge is entered.",
      "This form uses a gross return before its entered annual operating expense. If your return already includes that expense, use the Investment Fee calculator’s After fund fees basis rather than deducting it twice. These are hypothetical constant returns with no taxes or trading costs."
    ],
    "guidePath": "/guides/mutual-fund-fees-and-investment-costs/",
    "related": [
